{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":68325,"num_turns":2,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"f410f03e-aa90-505f-995f-b1c7738e97bb","total_cost_usd":0.536019,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9866,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6686,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9866,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6686,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9866,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9866},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":4284,"outputTokens":19,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004378999999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":6686,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9866,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.53164,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\":\"actor:dependence-free-batch-g2-reviewer-fable-34\",\"reviews\":[{\"case_id\":\"case:ff99e13110aad17a7fd0\",\"verdict\":\"demonstrated_issue\",\"issue_class\":\"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\",\"bounded_statement\":\"The selected result (Welch two-sample t-test on 48 TDS readings, 24 per grind group, t = 8.456, df = 45.1, p < 0.0001) enters each declared independent unit (vessel_id) into a row-independent two-sample t-test four times: the design has only 12 independent vessels (6 coarse, 6 fine), each sampled at four draw times, but every row was supplied to stats.ttest_ind as a separate independent observation. The four within-vessel readings are strongly correlated (TDS rises monotonically within every vessel), so the reported per-group n of 24 and df of 45.1 overstate the information content of the data and the p-value and standard errors of the selected result are anti-conservative relative to a vessel-level analysis with 6 units per group.\",\"root_cause\":\"workflow/analysis.py never references the independent-unit column vessel_id: tds_for() pools tds_percent from all rows matching a grind setting, and main() passes these pooled row-level arrays directly to stats.ttest_ind (a procedure that assumes each supplied value is an independent observation), with no per-vessel aggregation or model term accounting for repeated draws from the same vessel.\",\"evidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The data description declares vessel_id as the independent unit and states each vessel appears in four rows (one per draw time), with grind constant within a vessel — so rows are repeated measurements of the same unit, not independent observations.\",\"observed_value\":\"12 vessels x 4 draws = 48 rows; independent unit = vessel_id\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":22,\"end_line\":23,\"quoted_text\":\"One row is: one aliquot drawn from one steeping vessel at one draw time\\nIndependent unit column: vessel_id\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":13,\"end_line\":14,\"quoted_text\":\"- vessel_id: label of the steeping vessel, V01 through V12. Each vessel appears\\n  in four rows, one per draw time.\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":15,\"end_line\":17,\"quoted_text\":\"- grind: the grind setting loaded into that vessel, \\\"coarse\\\" or \\\"fine\\\". The\\n  setting is a property of the vessel, so it is the same in all four of a\\n  vessel's rows.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow pools all row-level TDS values by grind setting without any reference to vessel_id and feeds them to a two-sample t-test that treats each row as an independent observation; vessel_id is never used anywhere in analysis.py.\",\"observed_value\":\"stats.ttest_ind(fine, coarse, equal_var=False) applied to 24 row-level values per group from 6 vessels per group\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":26,\"end_line\":28,\"quoted_text\":\"def tds_for(table, setting):\\n    picked = [float(row[\\\"tds_percent\\\"]) for row in table if row[\\\"grind\\\"] == setting]\\n    return np.asarray(picked, dtype=float)\"},{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":48,\"end_line\":53,\"quoted_text\":\"    table = read_table(DATA_FILE)\\n    samples = {setting: tds_for(table, setting) for setting in SETTINGS}\\n    coarse = samples[\\\"coarse\\\"]\\n    fine = samples[\\\"fine\\\"]\\n\\n    tstat, pval = stats.ttest_ind(fine, coarse, equal_var=False)\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report's selected result claims per-group n of 24 and df of 45.1, and the Analysis section explicitly states every row was supplied to the test as one observation — confirming each of the 12 vessels entered the row-independent procedure four times.\",\"observed_value\":\"n = 48 rows (24 fine, 24 coarse), df = 45.1, p < 0.0001 versus 12 independent vessels\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":14,\"end_line\":17,\"quoted_text\":\"Welch two-sample t-test (two-sided, unequal variances) comparing the TDS\\nreadings recorded at the fine grind setting with those recorded at the\\ncoarse setting. Every row of the data file was supplied to the test as one\\nobservation.\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":24,\"end_line\":24,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Welch two-sample t-test on 48 TDS readings (24 fine, 24 coarse): mean difference fine minus coarse = 0.2521 percentage points, t = 8.456, df = 45.1, p < 0.0001.\"}]},{\"description\":\"Within-vessel readings are strongly positively correlated, not independent: TDS increases monotonically with steep time within every vessel (e.g., V01 rises 1.06 -> 1.13 -> 1.23 -> 1.26), so the four rows per vessel carry far less than four independent observations' worth of information about the grind effect.\",\"observed_value\":\"V01 tds_percent across draws: 1.06, 1.13, 1.23, 1.26 (monotone increasing; same pattern in all 12 vessels)\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":2,\"end_line\":5,\"quoted_text\":\"V01,coarse,6,4.1,1.06\\nV01,coarse,12,4.1,1.13\\nV01,coarse,18,4.1,1.23\\nV01,coarse,24,4.1,1.26\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The report transparently discloses the repeated-sampling design and the fact that every row was used as one observation, so the pooling is not concealed; moreover, per-vessel mean TDS values computed from the CSV separate the groups completely (coarse vessel means ~1.08 to 1.22 %, fine vessel means ~1.32 to 1.47 %, no overlap), so a correct vessel-level analysis with 6 units per group would likely still find a significant fine-versus-coarse difference in the same direction. This mitigates the practical consequence for the direction of the conclusion, but does not change the fact that the selected result's n, df, t and p were computed by entering each vessel four times into a procedure that assumes independent rows.\",\"observed_value\":\"coarse vessel means: 1.170, 1.090, 1.220, 1.120, 1.135, 1.080; fine vessel means: 1.3375, 1.4425, 1.3975, 1.315, 1.4675, 1.3675\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":5,\"end_line\":7,\"quoted_text\":\"48 total-dissolved-solids (TDS) readings taken from laboratory cold-brew\\nsteeping vessels. Each vessel was loaded with a single grind setting and\\nsampled at draw times of 6, 12, 18 and 24 hours.\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":26,\"end_line\":29,\"quoted_text\":\"V07,fine,6,4.2,1.21\\nV07,fine,12,4.2,1.31\\nV07,fine,18,4.2,1.37\\nV07,fine,24,4.2,1.46\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I attempted to falsify the finding in three ways. (1) Could the 48 rows legitimately be independent observations? No: the data description explicitly names vessel_id as the independent-unit column and states each vessel is sampled four times with grind fixed per vessel, and the CSV shows monotone within-vessel TDS trajectories confirming strong within-unit correlation. (2) Does the workflow aggregate to one value per vessel or otherwise model the repeated draws (e.g., vessel means, mixed model, clustered errors) anywhere? No: the string vessel_id appears nowhere in analysis.py; tds_for() filters rows only by grind and the pooled row-level arrays go straight into stats.ttest_ind. (3) Do the reported statistics correspond to a unit-level analysis by coincidence? No: the report states n = 24 per group and df = 45.1, which are only obtainable by treating all 48 rows as independent; a vessel-level Welch test would have n = 6 per group and at most 10 df. All three attempts failed to overturn the finding, so the issue is demonstrated. The only mitigating observation is that vessel-level means still separate the groups, so the direction of the conclusion is likely robust even though the selected result's inferential statistics are inflated.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-g2-reviewer-fable-34","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:ff99e13110aad17a7fd0","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The selected result (Welch two-sample t-test on 48 TDS readings, 24 per grind group, t = 8.456, df = 45.1, p < 0.0001) enters each declared independent unit (vessel_id) into a row-independent two-sample t-test four times: the design has only 12 independent vessels (6 coarse, 6 fine), each sampled at four draw times, but every row was supplied to stats.ttest_ind as a separate independent observation. The four within-vessel readings are strongly correlated (TDS rises monotonically within every vessel), so the reported per-group n of 24 and df of 45.1 overstate the information content of the data and the p-value and standard errors of the selected result are anti-conservative relative to a vessel-level analysis with 6 units per group.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py never references the independent-unit column vessel_id: tds_for() pools tds_percent from all rows matching a grind setting, and main() passes these pooled row-level arrays directly to stats.ttest_ind (a procedure that assumes each supplied value is an independent observation), with no per-vessel aggregation or model term accounting for repeated draws from the same vessel.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares vessel_id as the independent unit and states each vessel appears in four rows (one per draw time), with grind constant within a vessel — so rows are repeated measurements of the same unit, not independent observations.","observed_value":"12 vessels x 4 draws = 48 rows; independent unit = vessel_id","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":22,"end_line":23,"quoted_text":"One row is: one aliquot drawn from one steeping vessel at one draw time\nIndependent unit column: vessel_id"},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":13,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"- vessel_id: label of the steeping vessel, V01 through V12. Each vessel appears\n  in four rows, one per draw time."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":15,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"- grind: the grind setting loaded into that vessel, \"coarse\" or \"fine\". The\n  setting is a property of the vessel, so it is the same in all four of a\n  vessel's rows."}]},{"description":"The workflow pools all row-level TDS values by grind setting without any reference to vessel_id and feeds them to a two-sample t-test that treats each row as an independent observation; vessel_id is never used anywhere in analysis.py.","observed_value":"stats.ttest_ind(fine, coarse, equal_var=False) applied to 24 row-level values per group from 6 vessels per group","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":26,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"def tds_for(table, setting):\n    picked = [float(row[\"tds_percent\"]) for row in table if row[\"grind\"] == setting]\n    return np.asarray(picked, dtype=float)"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":48,"end_line":53,"quoted_text":"    table = read_table(DATA_FILE)\n    samples = {setting: tds_for(table, setting) for setting in SETTINGS}\n    coarse = samples[\"coarse\"]\n    fine = samples[\"fine\"]\n\n    tstat, pval = stats.ttest_ind(fine, coarse, equal_var=False)"}]},{"description":"The report's selected result claims per-group n of 24 and df of 45.1, and the Analysis section explicitly states every row was supplied to the test as one observation — confirming each of the 12 vessels entered the row-independent procedure four times.","observed_value":"n = 48 rows (24 fine, 24 coarse), df = 45.1, p < 0.0001 versus 12 independent vessels","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":14,"end_line":17,"quoted_text":"Welch two-sample t-test (two-sided, unequal variances) comparing the TDS\nreadings recorded at the fine grind setting with those recorded at the\ncoarse setting. Every row of the data file was supplied to the test as one\nobservation."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":24,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Welch two-sample t-test on 48 TDS readings (24 fine, 24 coarse): mean difference fine minus coarse = 0.2521 percentage points, t = 8.456, df = 45.1, p < 0.0001."}]},{"description":"Within-vessel readings are strongly positively correlated, not independent: TDS increases monotonically with steep time within every vessel (e.g., V01 rises 1.06 -> 1.13 -> 1.23 -> 1.26), so the four rows per vessel carry far less than four independent observations' worth of information about the grind effect.","observed_value":"V01 tds_percent across draws: 1.06, 1.13, 1.23, 1.26 (monotone increasing; same pattern in all 12 vessels)","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"V01,coarse,6,4.1,1.06\nV01,coarse,12,4.1,1.13\nV01,coarse,18,4.1,1.23\nV01,coarse,24,4.1,1.26"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report transparently discloses the repeated-sampling design and the fact that every row was used as one observation, so the pooling is not concealed; moreover, per-vessel mean TDS values computed from the CSV separate the groups completely (coarse vessel means ~1.08 to 1.22 %, fine vessel means ~1.32 to 1.47 %, no overlap), so a correct vessel-level analysis with 6 units per group would likely still find a significant fine-versus-coarse difference in the same direction. This mitigates the practical consequence for the direction of the conclusion, but does not change the fact that the selected result's n, df, t and p were computed by entering each vessel four times into a procedure that assumes independent rows.","observed_value":"coarse vessel means: 1.170, 1.090, 1.220, 1.120, 1.135, 1.080; fine vessel means: 1.3375, 1.4425, 1.3975, 1.315, 1.4675, 1.3675","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"48 total-dissolved-solids (TDS) readings taken from laboratory cold-brew\nsteeping vessels. Each vessel was loaded with a single grind setting and\nsampled at draw times of 6, 12, 18 and 24 hours."},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":26,"end_line":29,"quoted_text":"V07,fine,6,4.2,1.21\nV07,fine,12,4.2,1.31\nV07,fine,18,4.2,1.37\nV07,fine,24,4.2,1.46"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the finding in three ways. (1) Could the 48 rows legitimately be independent observations? No: the data description explicitly names vessel_id as the independent-unit column and states each vessel is sampled four times with grind fixed per vessel, and the CSV shows monotone within-vessel TDS trajectories confirming strong within-unit correlation. (2) Does the workflow aggregate to one value per vessel or otherwise model the repeated draws (e.g., vessel means, mixed model, clustered errors) anywhere? No: the string vessel_id appears nowhere in analysis.py; tds_for() filters rows only by grind and the pooled row-level arrays go straight into stats.ttest_ind. (3) Do the reported statistics correspond to a unit-level analysis by coincidence? No: the report states n = 24 per group and df = 45.1, which are only obtainable by treating all 48 rows as independent; a vessel-level Welch test would have n = 6 per group and at most 10 df. All three attempts failed to overturn the finding, so the issue is demonstrated. The only mitigating observation is that vessel-level means still separate the groups, so the direction of the conclusion is likely robust even though the selected result's inferential statistics are inflated.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":29577,"ttft_stream_ms":1766,"time_to_request_ms":14,"type":"result","duration_ms":68583,"uuid":"2518a4de-f849-4123-8ef8-9eb98ad8d488"}
